narrative:
## Building Your Safe Type Arsenal

Small, composable safe types form the foundation of any robust system. Each type does one validation, one invariant:

```xiom
type PositiveInt = Int
  invariant: this > 0;

type NonEmptyVec[T] = {
  data: Vec[T];
  invariant: data.len() > 0;
}

type ValidEmail = {
  address: Str;
  invariant: address.contains("@");
  invariant: address.contains(".");
}
```

Each type is independently validated. Combined, they form a library:

```xiom
fn send_welcome(to: &ValidEmail, name: &NonEmptyStr) {
  io.println("Welcome " + name.value + "!");
  io.println("Sending to: " + to.address);
}
```

The function `send_welcome` doesn't validate anything — the types guarantee validity. No `if email.contains("@")`, no `if name == ""`.

### The Power of Composition
```xiom
type ValidPhone = {
  number: Str;
  invariant: string.str_len(number) >= 10;
  invariant: string.str_len(number) <= 15;
}

fn contact(user_email: &ValidEmail, user_phone: &ValidPhone) {
  io.println("Contact: " + user_email.address + " or " + user_phone.number);
}
```

Build one safe type, use it in dozens of functions. The validation cost pays off every time a function skips a redundant check.

